Literal pool value-dedup across banks
// am1 pools a bank's literals BY VALUE. Two literals whose 12-bit values
// coincide therefore share ONE pool word, even when they mean addresses in
// different banks -- which is correct: the word is the same 18 bits either
// way, and which bank an indirect through it resolves to is decided by the
// extend-mode state at execution, not by the pool.
//
// The consequence is that a bank's pool can grow or shrink by a word when
// some OTHER bank's addresses move and a coincidence is made or broken.
// So a change entirely local to one bank can move another bank's pool: a
// bank-0 symbol moving from 006230 to 006277 stops colliding with a bank-2
// address at offset 6230, and bank 2's pool grows by a word though nothing
// in bank 2 changed.
//
//   am1 -l -DALPHAORG=2000 litpool_bankcollide.am1   -> ONE pool word
//   am1 -l -DALPHAORG=2001 litpool_bankcollide.am1   -> TWO pool words
//
// The same two runs check the listing. A pooled literal must be labeled with
// what its own reference wrote, not with the expression interned into the
// slot FIRST: that would ignore both the bank qualifier and the source's
// text, and in the ALPHAORG=2000 run print `lac [beta` as `lac [alpha:0]`.
// The generated code would be right and only the annotation would lie, as a
// subroutine call labeled with an unrelated string in another bank would,
// which is exactly the kind of thing that sends someone chasing a phantom
// through a listing. The parser keeps each
// reference's own expression tree on the CONSTANT node (value2.ptr) and the
// listing prints from there.
//
// The check: in BOTH runs line 43 must print `lac [alpha:0]` and line 44 must
// print `lac [beta]`. The ALPHAORG=2000 run must still show them sharing one
// pool word -- identical object words, different labels. Regression test
// rg34_litpool_labels in Tests/Regression pins exactly that.
100/
    hlt

// Bank 0's alpha is placed by -DALPHAORG. Bank 1's beta is fixed at 2000.
// Both symbols are DEFINED before the two literals below reference them,
// so both values are known when the pool slot is chosen. (With forward
// references am1 allocates separate slots and no dedup happens -- that is
// worth knowing too: a test written with forward references here would
// show nothing.)
bank 0
ALPHAORG/
alpha, 0

bank 1
2000/
beta, 0

3000/
    lac [alpha:0        // FAR literal  -- bank 0's 12-bit address
    lac [beta           // NEAR literal -- bank 1's own 12-bit offset
    hlt

constants

bank 0
constants
start 100
